BEGIN_PGML Suppose [`A`] and [`B`] are positive constants such that [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}0 \lt A \lt AB \lt 1 \lt B\text{.}`] Arrange the following quantities from smallest to largest. a. [`0`]
a. [`\ln A`]
a. [`\ln B`]
a. [`\ln A + \ln B`]
a. [`\ln (B^A)`]
[_]{$popup1}{5} [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}\lt`] [_]{$popup2}{5} [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}\lt`] [_]{$popup3}{5} [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}\lt`] [_]{$popup4}{5} [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}\lt`] [_]{$popup5}{5}
END_PGML
############################################################ # Hint ############################################################ #Set value of $showHint in PGcourse.pl for course-wide attempt threshhold for revealing hints
BEGIN_PGML_HINT Consider using Logarithm Laws, and also use the graph of [`y=\ln x\text{.}`] For which values of [`x`] is [`\ln x`] positive? When is it negative? Can you see from the graph which values of [`x`] make [`\ln x`] larger/smaller?
END_PGML_HINT
